  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 235 PM EST Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and unseasonably warm conditions continue \nthrough tomorrow.\n\nHigh pressure remains in control of the weather across the region \nthis afternoon, with dry conditions and mostly sunny skies \nprevailing. Temperatures have warmed well into the 70s across \nmuch off the area, several degrees above normal for early \nNovember. High pressure shifts farther offshore tonight, with \nmostly clear skies and calm winds expected. Another round of \npatchy fog will be possible late tonight into early Wednesday \nmorning as favorable radiational cooling conditions develop. Low\ntemperatures will not be as cool as last night, but we will see\nreadings ranging from the upper 40s to lower 50s. \n\nRemaining dry and mostly sunny on Wednesday with highs ranging from \nthe mid 70s to around 80 (slightly cooler along the immediate \ncoast). \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Widespread moderate to locally heavy rainfall is \nexpected Thursday night through Friday, with a few thunderstorms \npossible. \n\nHigh pressure continues to move offshore Thursday as an upper-level \ntrough and associated cold front approach from the west. Expect \nanother warm day with increasing cloud cover. A few showers cannot \nbe ruled out, especially across western portions of the area, but \nthe more widespread rainfall is expected to hold off until Thursday \nnight. \n\nConfidence continues to increase in widespread rainfall late \nThursday night through Friday, with the latest 12z guidance \ngenerally trending wetter. Both the GFS and ECMWF depict a southwest \nto northeast axis of enhanced QPF extending from south-central VA up \nto the MD Eastern Shore. The GEFS and EPS have also trended wetter, \nwith the CMC remains somewhat drier. Overall rainfall amounts \nof 0.50\" to 1.50\" appear increasingly likely, with locally \nhigher amounts in excess of 2\" or more possible across the \nNorthern Neck over to the MD Eastern Shore. The 12z EPS \nindicates probabilities of exceeding 1\" ranging from 40-50% \nacross central and south-central VA to 60-70% over the MD \nEastern Shore. Meanwhile, probabilities of exceeding 2\" remain \nconsiderably lower (~10-20%), lending lower confidence to the \nhigher-end rainfall amounts. \n\nAnomalously high moisture will accompany this system, with PWATs \nforecast to surge to 200%+ of normal for this time of year. This \nwill support locally heavy rainfall, especially with any embedded \nconvection. Have maintained a slight chance of thunderstorms \nThursday night through Friday, with some elevated instability \npossible Thursday night and modest surface based instability \ndeveloping Friday afternoon (SE). The 12z ECMWF depicts MLCAPE \nvalues of ~500-750 J/kg across SE VA Friday afternoon. In addition \nshear increases to ~40-50 knots. While this environment may support \nthe potential for a few stronger storms, widespread clouds and \nongoing precipitation may limit destabilization, keeping \nconfidence in any severe weather threat low at this time. Rain \nchances should diminish from west to east Friday evening as the \ncold front moves offshore. Pleasant and drier weather returns \nfor the weekend.",
